{
  // Repository tooling lives outside the application build: `scripts/` and
  // `.claude/hooks/` are plain JavaScript so npm, CI and Claude Code can run
  // them with no compile step and no `npm ci`. That is a deliberate exception
  // to "TypeScript, no exceptions" in AGENTS.md, and this config is what keeps
  // it from also being an exception to type checking — `checkJs` holds the
  // JSDoc annotations to the same strict rules the application is held to.
  // Both directories are listed because the decision that granted the
  // exception granted it to both, conditional on exactly this file covering
  // them. `.claude/hooks/` was left out when this config was first written, so
  // the condition lived in the decision and not in the build — and the first
  // run that did cover it reported an implicit `any` for every declared
  // function parameter in both files — and, through them, for the inline
  // callbacks whose receiver came from one.
  //
  // Extends the application config rather than restating it, so a future
  // `target` bump or new strictness flag reaches both. Only the four options
  // that must differ are overridden: `rootDir`, because the base pins it to
  // ./src and a file outside that is an error; and allowJs/checkJs/noEmit,
  // which describe what this directory is.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "rootDir": ".",
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  // The root config files are here for the same reason `.claude/hooks/**` is:
  // they configure this repository and no `tsc` invocation was reading them.
  // The application config is pinned to `src/**/*`, this one covered only the
  // two tooling directories, and between them `drizzle.config.ts` — which
  // parses env with Zod and names the schema sources a migration is generated
  // from — was never type checked at all. Neither was `vitest.config.ts`,
  // which decides what `npm test` runs.
  //
  // Two of the three are TypeScript; `eslint.config.js` is not, and it is the
  // reason the paragraph above says "configure this repository" rather than
  // "are TypeScript". It reaches the program through the same allowJs/checkJs
  // pair as `scripts/` and `.claude/hooks/`, which widens the "plain
  // JavaScript" exception to a file outside both directories the original
  // decision named. That widening is itself recorded — docs/DECISIONS.md →
  // "Перевірка шляхів у конфігах тулінгу" — so this include list stays
  // explainable by a decision rather than by whoever edited it last.
  //
  // Listed as three explicit files, not a glob: the repository root also holds
  // `docker-compose.yml` and dotfiles, and a pattern loose enough to catch
  // future config would catch whatever else lands beside them. The cost of an
  // explicit list is that it drifts silently when a fourth config lands, so
  // `npm run check:config-paths` fails the build when a root `*.config.*` file
  // is in neither tsconfig.
  "include": [
    "scripts/**/*",
    ".claude/hooks/**/*",
    "drizzle.config.ts",
    "vitest.config.ts",
    "eslint.config.js"
  ]
}
